from a_common import P,pt,table,svg,txt,line,box

def build(B):
    c='A2.1'
    B.add(c,'ahl','A compartment that is not yet a cell','A laboratory vesicle encloses organic molecules and grows when additional lipid is supplied. It cannot copy information or maintain a network of energy-releasing reactions.',[P('Explain why growth alone does not show that the vesicle is alive.','Non-living assemblies can increase in size by adding material.','The vesicle lacks demonstrated self-replication of hereditary information and self-sustaining metabolism.'),P('State one useful role that this vesicle could nevertheless model in cell origins.','It models compartmentalization, which can keep internal chemistry distinct from the surroundings.')],statements='A2.1.2-3, A2.1.5')
    B.add(c,'ahl','Testing an abiotic synthesis model','A constructed experiment compares sealed gas mixtures exposed to electrical sparks. All mixtures initially contain no amino acids.'+table(['Treatment','Spark','Amino acids detected after 5 days / arbitrary units'],[['Mixture A','yes',38],['Mixture A','no',2],['Mixture B','yes',14]]),[P('Calculate the difference between the sparked and unsparked samples of mixture A.','The difference is $38-2=36$ arbitrary units.'),P('Explain the conclusion supported by this comparison.','Under the tested conditions, electrical energy is associated with a greater yield of amino acids.'),P('Explain why the result does not establish the exact atmosphere of early Earth.','The experiment tests selected gas compositions; it does not independently show which gases or concentrations actually existed on early Earth.')],data=True,statements='A2.1.1, A2.1.4')
    B.add(c,'ahl','Controlling contamination in an origin experiment','A researcher reports organic molecules after heating a gas mixture in a glass apparatus. Which control most directly tests whether residues in the apparatus explain the result?',correct='Run a cleaned apparatus blank without the proposed carbon-containing reactants and analyse it in the same way.',wrong=['Increase the heating time only in the experimental apparatus.','Use the same sample but report its concentration in different units.','Add a known amino acid to the mixture before the experiment.'],why='A blank can reveal organic contamination from equipment or processing; the other changes do not isolate that explanation.',statements='A2.1.4')
    B.add(c,'ahl','Interpreting a successful synthesis','A headline states: “Amino acids produced from simple gases prove that living cells appeared in one step.”',[P('Identify the unsupported part of the claim.','Production of amino acids does not demonstrate production of a living cell.'),P('Explain two additional requirements for a first cellular system.','A system must preserve and replicate hereditary information.','Compartmentalization and a sustained catalytic reaction network are also needed; credit either with its role.')],statements='A2.1.3-4')
    vesicle=svg(txt(410,30,'Fatty-acid vesicle model: cross-section')+'<circle cx="410" cy="180" r="100" fill="#e8f4fb" stroke="#43798a" stroke-width="10"/><circle cx="410" cy="180" r="86" fill="none" stroke="#78a9b3" stroke-width="6"/>'+txt(410,174,'Internal solution',19)+line(160,130,312,140,arrow=True)+txt(150,115,'Bilayer',17)+txt(410,320,'Constructed schematic; not to scale',15))
    B.add(c,'ahl','Why a bilayer can form a boundary','Fatty acids with a polar region and a non-polar region are mixed with water and form vesicles.',[P('Explain why the molecules assemble into bilayers.','Polar regions interact with water while non-polar regions associate away from water.','A bilayer places non-polar regions inside and polar regions facing the internal and external aqueous solutions.'),P('Explain one advantage of a closed vesicle over an open sheet for an early reaction system.','It encloses a distinct space, allowing some reactants or products to accumulate and internal conditions to differ.')],diagram=vesicle,statements='A2.1.5')
    B.add(c,'ahl','Benefits and costs of an early boundary','Constructed measurements show that a molecule escapes from an unbounded reaction mixture with a half-time of 2 minutes but from a vesicle with a half-time of 20 minutes.',[P('Calculate the factor by which the half-time increases.','The increase is $20/2=10$ fold.'),P('Explain one potential benefit and one potential cost of the vesicle boundary.','Retention can keep catalytic molecules and reaction intermediates near each other.','The boundary may also restrict entry of substrates or removal of waste, so it must permit sufficient exchange.')],data=True,statements='A2.1.3, A2.1.5')
    B.add(c,'ahl','Why RNA is a candidate for early inheritance','Which combination most strongly explains the proposed dual role of RNA in early life?',correct='RNA can carry sequence information, and some RNA molecules catalyse reactions.',wrong=['RNA contains no sequence information and is always chemically inert.','RNA makes a lipid bilayer without other molecules and supplies unlimited energy.','RNA must be enclosed in a modern nucleus before any interaction is possible.'],why='RNA can serve informational and catalytic roles, reducing the need for separate DNA and protein systems at the earliest stages.',statements='A2.1.6')
    B.add(c,'ahl','Testing a catalytic molecule','A constructed preparation catalyses a reaction. Activity is measured after treatment with enzymes that selectively degrade RNA or protein. All treatments are otherwise identical.'+table(['Treatment','Remaining activity / %'],[['No degrading enzyme',100],['RNA-degrading enzyme',8],['Protein-degrading enzyme',94]]),[P('Deduce which type of molecule is the stronger candidate for the catalyst.','RNA is the stronger candidate because destroying RNA nearly eliminates activity while destroying protein has little effect.'),P('Suggest a control needed before accepting that conclusion.','Check that both degrading enzymes were active on known RNA/protein substrates and did not otherwise inhibit the test reaction.'),P('Explain the relevance to an RNA-world hypothesis.','It illustrates that catalytic activity need not depend only on proteins, making an early informational-and-catalytic RNA system plausible.')],data=True,statements='A2.1.6')
    B.add(c,'ahl','A possible division of molecular labour','For this question, assume DNA preserves sequence information more stably than RNA, while proteins can provide a wider range of catalytic activities. An early RNA-based system gradually gains DNA and protein components.',[P('Suggest an advantage of retaining hereditary information in DNA.','More stable storage could reduce loss or damage of sequence information between generations.'),P('Suggest why RNA might remain important after this transition.','RNA can connect stored DNA information to protein production and can retain catalytic roles such as in ribosomes.'),P('Explain why these advantages do not prove that the proposed historical sequence occurred.','A plausible functional explanation is not direct evidence of the actual sequence of evolutionary events.')],statements='A2.1.6')
    B.add(c,'ahl','Shared machinery in distant lineages','Comparisons find related ribosomal components and a broadly shared genetic code in bacteria, archaea and eukaryotes.',[P('Explain how this supports a last universal common ancestor.','Shared complex molecular features can be inherited from an ancestral population common to the three lineages.'),P('Explain why this does not require all present-day organisms to have identical genomes.','Mutations, gene gains/losses and divergence after the ancestor can generate differences while conserving fundamental machinery.')],statements='A2.1.7')
    B.add(c,'ahl','First life and the last common ancestor','A student states that the last universal common ancestor must have been the first living entity. Which correction is justified?',correct='Earlier lineages may have existed and become extinct without leaving living descendants.',wrong=['The last universal common ancestor must be a species alive today.','Every earlier lineage must still exist today.','A common ancestor cannot have any descendants with different traits.'],why='LUCA concerns ancestry shared by living organisms; extinct earlier branches need not be represented among living descendants.',statements='A2.1.7-8')
    B.add(c,'ahl','Dating the minimum age of life',r'A constructed geological report dates a rock layer containing putative biological structures to $3.45\pm0.05$ billion years ago. The authors argue that the structures formed at the same time as the rock.',[P('State the age interval implied by the measurement.','3.40 to 3.50 billion years ago.'),P('Explain what the find could establish if both the biological origin and dating are secure.','Life existed by approximately the age of that layer; it establishes an ancient occurrence rather than the exact time life first originated.'),P('Identify one additional issue that must be evaluated.','Whether the structures are genuinely biological rather than mineral artefacts, or whether they entered the rock later; credit either.')],data=True,statements='A2.1.8-9')
    B.add(c,'ahl','Interpreting a molecular date','A constructed analysis estimates a common ancestor at 3.8 billion years ago, but alternative evolutionary-rate assumptions produce estimates from 3.4 to 4.1 billion years ago.',[P('Explain why a single reported date may be misleading.','The estimate depends substantially on rate assumptions, so its uncertainty is wider than the single date suggests.'),P('Suggest an independent type of evidence that could help constrain the estimate.','Well-dated geological or fossil evidence of life can provide independent chronological constraints.'),P('Distinguish the age of a common ancestor from the age of the first cells.','The first cells could predate the ancestor shared by surviving lineages.')],data=True,statements='A2.1.8')
    B.add(c,'ahl','Assessing a hydrothermal-vent hypothesis','A researcher finds microscopic structures in ancient vent deposits and conserved genes associated with chemical energy use in organisms. They propose that an early ancestor lived near hydrothermal vents.',[P('Explain how the two observations provide different kinds of evidence.','The deposits provide geological evidence of possible ancient life in a vent environment.','Conserved genes provide molecular evidence about inherited biochemical capabilities.'),P('Give one limitation of the inference.','The structures may have a non-biological explanation, or conserved genes may not identify one unique habitat; credit either with its implication.')],statements='A2.1.9')
    B.add(c,'ahl','Why only one ancestral lineage is detectable','An early Earth model produces several self-replicating systems. One uses resources more efficiently and its descendants spread.',[P('Suggest how competition could make one system ancestral to all organisms alive today.','More successful descendants could outcompete other systems, whose lineages then become extinct.'),P('Explain why the absence of living descendants of other systems is not proof that they never existed.','Extinction removes living evidence, and early systems may have left no recognizable fossils.')],statements='A2.1.7')
    B.add(c,'ahl','Making an origin hypothesis testable','A hypothesis proposes that enclosing a catalytic RNA in fatty-acid vesicles improves its persistence through repeated dilution.',[P('Outline a controlled comparison.','Compare equivalent catalytic RNA amounts in vesicles and without vesicles under the same dilution schedule.','Keep initial RNA concentration, temperature and solution conditions constant and use replicates.'),P('State a measurable outcome and a result that would challenge the hypothesis.','Measure retained catalytic activity or intact RNA after dilution; no greater persistence in vesicles would challenge the prediction.')],statements='A2.1.3, A2.1.5-6')

    c='A2.2'
    B.add(c,'sl','Reading a generalized plant-cell cutaway','The accompanying original schematic represents a photosynthetic plant cell. It is not a micrograph and is not to scale. Structures are labelled A–E.',[P('Identify structures A, B and C.','A: cell wall.','B: chloroplast.','C: nucleus.'),P('State one function of D and one function of E.','D, the central vacuole, stores cell sap and contributes to turgor/support; credit either function.','E, the mitochondrion, carries out aerobic respiration and produces ATP.'),P('Explain one limitation of using this image as evidence about an actual cell.','It is a generalized constructed illustration; its colours, dimensions or organelle numbers are not direct measurements of a specimen.')],statements='A2.2.6, A2.2.8, A2.2.11',notes='Reserved for root-owned original plant-cell cutaway. A wall, B chloroplast, C nucleus, D vacuole, E mitochondrion. No inline diagram. Letter answers checked against actual image; no magnification inference.')
    scale=svg(txt(410,30,'Constructed cell outline and scale bar')+'<ellipse cx="400" cy="155" rx="200" ry="74" fill="#e4f0f5" stroke="#305b74" stroke-width="3"/>'+line(200,255,600,255,colour='#a75d28',arrow=False)+txt(400,245,'Cell length',16)+line(600,300,700,300,width=5)+txt(650,328,'10 μm',17)+txt(410,355,'Schematic; lengths are proportional to the scale bar',15),820,380)
    B.add(c,'sl','Using a scale bar after image resizing',r'In the constructed diagram, the cell is four times as long as the $10\,\mu\mathrm m$ scale bar.',[P('Determine the represented cell length.',r'$4\times10=40\,\mu\mathrm m$.'),P('Explain why the scale bar remains useful when the whole image is enlarged proportionally.','The cell and bar enlarge by the same factor, so their length ratio stays unchanged.')],diagram=scale,statements='A2.2.2')
    B.add(c,'sl','Calibrating an eyepiece graticule',r'Constructed calibration data show that 40 eyepiece divisions align with $100\,\mu\mathrm m$ on a stage micrometer. A cell spans 18 eyepiece divisions at the same objective setting.',[P('Calculate the length represented by one eyepiece division.',r'$100/40=2.5\,\mu\mathrm m$ per division.'),P('Calculate the cell length.',r'$18\times2.5=45\,\mu\mathrm m$.'),P('Explain why a new calibration is needed after changing the objective.','The objective changes image magnification, so a division corresponds to a different actual distance.')],data=True,statements='A2.2.2')
    B.add(c,'sl','Enlargement without new detail','A low-resolution image is digitally enlarged fivefold, but two close structures still appear as one blurred spot. Which explanation is correct?',correct='Enlargement increases displayed size but does not recover unresolved structural detail.',wrong=['Digital enlargement must increase optical resolving power by fivefold.','The two structures must have fused biologically during enlargement.','Magnification and resolution are always the same quantity.'],why='Resolution is the ability to distinguish nearby details; interpolating more pixels cannot create information absent from the original image.',statements='A2.2.2-3')
    B.add(c,'sl','Calculating magnification',r'A printed image of a cell is $36\,\mathrm{mm}$ long. The actual cell length is $24\,\mu\mathrm m$.',[P('Calculate the image magnification.',pt(r'$36\,\mathrm{mm}=36\,000\,\mu\mathrm m$ and magnification is image size divided by actual size.','M1'),r'Magnification is $36\,000/24=1500$, or $\times1500$.'),P('State why unit conversion is necessary.','The ratio must compare lengths in the same unit to produce a dimensionless magnification.')],statements='A2.2.2')
    B.add(c,'sl','Staining and a fair comparison','A student compares stained onion cells with unstained cells. The stained preparation also uses a stronger objective lens and a thicker tissue sample.',[P('Explain why the effect of staining cannot be isolated.','Objective magnification/resolution and specimen thickness change at the same time as staining, so either could affect visible detail.'),P('Outline two improvements.','Use equivalent thin tissue preparations and the same objective/illumination settings.','Prepare matched stained and unstained samples and inspect several fields for each.')],statements='A2.2.2-3')
    B.add(c,'sl','Recognizing common cellular features','An unfamiliar unicellular organism has no membrane-bound nucleus. Which combination is still expected in a typical living cell?',correct='A plasma membrane, cytoplasm, genetic material and ribosomes.',wrong=['A chloroplast, cell wall and large central vacuole in every case.','A membrane-bound nucleus and mitochondria in every case.','Only a protein capsid surrounding nucleic acid, with no cytoplasm.'],why='A typical prokaryotic or eukaryotic cell has a membrane-enclosed cytoplasm, hereditary material and protein-synthesis machinery.',statements='A2.2.4-6')
    B.add(c,'sl','Inferring cell organization','Constructed observations describe sample X with a nucleoid, a cell wall and 70S cytoplasmic ribosomes; sample Y has a nucleus, mitochondria and 80S cytoplasmic ribosomes.',[P('Identify X and Y as prokaryotic or eukaryotic.','X is prokaryotic; Y is eukaryotic.'),P('Explain why the cell wall alone would not distinguish the two groups.','Both bacterial cells and some eukaryotic cells, such as plants and fungi, have cell walls.'),P('State one additional structure consistent with a photosynthetic plant cell within group Y.','A chloroplast; credit a large sap vacuole if clearly tied to plant organization rather than universal presence.')],data=True,statements='A2.2.5-8')
    B.add(c,'sl','Distinguishing fungal and plant cells','A cell has a nucleus, a cell wall composed mainly of chitin and no chloroplasts.',[P('Identify the most likely broad group.','Fungi.'),P('Compare one structural feature with a typical photosynthetic plant cell.','Both have a nucleus and cell wall, but a plant wall is mainly cellulose rather than chitin.','A typical photosynthetic plant cell has chloroplasts, whereas the fungal cell does not.')],statements='A2.2.8')
    B.add(c,'sl','Exceptions to a one-nucleus model','A student claims that every eukaryotic cell has exactly one nucleus.',[P('Use two examples to evaluate the claim.','Mature mammalian erythrocytes have no nucleus, so the claim is not universal.','A skeletal muscle fibre or an aseptate fungal hypha can contain many nuclei, providing a second counterexample.'),P('Explain why such examples do not overturn cell theory.','Cell theory does not require every specialized cell to have exactly one nucleus; these structures arise within cellular organisms.')],statements='A2.2.1, A2.2.9')
    B.add(c,'sl','Life functions in one cell','A unicellular protist moves toward a food source, encloses food particles, digests them and divides.',[P('Identify the life function illustrated by movement toward the food source.','Response to stimuli; credit movement if the response is clearly distinguished from directed sensing elsewhere.'),P('Identify a second function and connect it to the observation.','Nutrition is illustrated by taking in/digesting food, or reproduction by division; credit either correctly linked.'),P('Explain why one cell can be a complete organism.','A unicellular organism performs the essential life functions within that one cell.')],statements='A2.2.7')
    B.add(c,'sl','Interpreting fluorescent staining','In a constructed fluorescence experiment, an antibody-linked dye binds a chosen cellular protein. Treated cells show bright puncta; a sample exposed only to the dye also shows a weak diffuse glow.',[P('Explain what the bright puncta could indicate.','They could mark regions where the target protein is concentrated.'),P('Explain the value of the dye-only comparison.','It reveals background or nonspecific fluorescence that should not be assigned to the target protein.'),P('State why fluorescence alone cannot give the complete ultrastructure of the cell.','It reveals the labelled target and background rather than all cellular components.')],data=True,statements='A2.2.3')
    B.add(c,'ahl','Evidence for an organelle origin','A constructed comparison records a nucleus-free organelle with circular DNA, 70S ribosomes and division by splitting.',[P('Explain how these features support endosymbiosis.','The features resemble bacterial hereditary and protein-synthesis systems.','Replication by splitting is consistent with descent from an incorporated prokaryotic cell.'),P('Explain why the organelle is not necessarily an independently living bacterium today.','Modern organelles depend on the host cell for many proteins, metabolites and regulatory functions.')],data=True,statements='A2.2.12')
    B.add(c,'ahl','Distinguishing evidence from an experiment’s side effects','A research compound inhibits 70S ribosomes. In isolated plant cells, protein production in chloroplasts decreases while cytoplasmic production initially changes little.',[P('Explain why the result fits an endosymbiotic origin.','Chloroplast ribosomes share the 70S type associated with bacteria, unlike the 80S cytoplasmic ribosomes.'),P('Explain why this result alone is insufficient to establish the evolutionary origin.','A compound’s effects can have alternative explanations; multiple independent structural and genetic observations are needed.')],statements='A2.2.12')
    B.add(c,'ahl','Same genome, different expression','Constructed data show that cell types X and Y from one organism have the same DNA sequence at two genes. Gene1 mRNA is abundant only in X; gene2 mRNA is abundant only in Y.',[P('Explain how the cells can develop different functions despite these DNA similarities.','Different genes are expressed in the two cell types.','The resulting differences in proteins can produce distinct structures and activities.'),P('State one possible trigger for a change in the expression pattern.','A developmental signal or environmental change can alter which genes are expressed.')],data=True,statements='A2.2.13')
    B.add(c,'ahl','Evaluating a multicellular organization','A hypothetical lineage evolves from separate cells to an attached group with some cells specialized for movement and others for nutrient capture.',[P('Explain two potential benefits.','Division of labour can make different functions more effective.','The group can attain a larger body size than one cell alone.'),P('Suggest one challenge created by this organization.','Cells need coordination and exchange of materials so that specialized cells receive resources they no longer acquire independently.')],statements='A2.2.14')

    c='A2.3'
    B.add(c,'ahl','Identifying a viral particle','Which observation most clearly distinguishes a typical viral particle from a small bacterial cell?',correct='Nucleic acid is enclosed by a capsid, with no cytoplasm or ribosomes.',wrong=['The particle is too small to be seen with the unaided eye.','The particle contains genetic information.','The particle can evolve over generations.'],why='Small size, genes and evolution occur in both groups; the acellular capsid organization and lack of cellular machinery distinguish the virus.',statements='A2.3.1')
    B.add(c,'ahl','Comparing viral structures','Constructed descriptions identify virus X as enveloped with RNA and virus Y as non-enveloped with double-stranded DNA.',[P('State two differences supported by the descriptions.','X has a surrounding envelope while Y does not.','X carries RNA whereas Y carries double-stranded DNA.'),P('State a structural component that both are expected to possess.','A protein capsid associated with the genetic material.')],data=True,statements='A2.3.1-2')
    stages=svg(txt(410,30,'Lambda-phage infection: processes to order')+box(45,75,330,70,'P: new phages assembled')+box(445,75,330,70,'Q: phage DNA enters cell')+box(45,205,330,70,'R: host cell lyses')+box(445,205,330,70,'S: viral DNA and proteins made')+txt(410,335,'Constructed schematic; letters do not indicate time order',15))
    B.add(c,'ahl','Ordering a lytic sequence','The diagram lists four processes after a lambda phage first contacts a susceptible bacterium.',[P('Put the processes in a biologically plausible order.','Q → S → P → R.'),P('Explain why assembly must follow production of viral components.','New particles need replicated genomes and synthesized capsid proteins before they can be assembled.'),P('Explain the effect of lysis on the infection.','Lysis releases progeny phages that can contact further host cells.')],diagram=stages,statements='A2.3.3')
    B.add(c,'ahl','Estimating a phage burst','In a constructed single-cycle experiment, 200 infected bacterial cells each release an average of 60 infectious phages. Assume all counted phages came from those cells and no second infection cycle occurred.',[P('Calculate the total infectious phage yield.',r'$200\times60=12\,000$ infectious phages.'),P('Explain why counting yield after several cycles would not measure the burst from the original cells alone.','Descendant phages could infect new cells and contribute additional progeny.')],data=True,statements='A2.3.3')
    B.add(c,'ahl','A quiet viral genome','A bacterial population carries lambda DNA integrated into its chromosomes. The bacteria divide without immediate lysis.',[P('Identify the viral state and cycle.','Integrated viral DNA is a prophage in the lysogenic cycle.'),P('Explain how viral information reaches daughter cells.','The prophage is copied with the bacterial chromosome and inherited during cell division.'),P('Explain why absence of lysis does not show that viral DNA is absent.','The genome can persist in the host in a non-lytic state.')],statements='A2.3.4')
    B.add(c,'ahl','Inducing a lysogenic population','Constructed results compare lysogenic cultures after a treatment that damages DNA. The treated culture releases many phages and its intact-cell count falls; the untreated culture remains stable.',[P('Suggest a change in viral activity consistent with both results.','Prophages are induced to enter the lytic cycle, producing phages and lysing host cells.'),P('Identify a control needed to separate virus-mediated lysis from direct toxicity.','Apply the same treatment to otherwise comparable bacteria lacking the prophage.'),P('Explain the possible value of the control result.','If those cells remain intact, direct treatment toxicity alone is less likely to explain the lysis.')],data=True,statements='A2.3.3-4')
    B.add(c,'ahl','Dependence on a host','A purified viral preparation contains genomes and capsids but produces no progeny in a nutrient solution without cells.',[P('Explain two host functions needed for viral production.','Host ribosomes and associated machinery synthesize viral proteins.','Host metabolism supplies energy and raw materials for genome replication and particle assembly.'),P('Explain why adding nutrients alone may not replace the host.','Nutrients do not supply the organized cellular machinery required to use those materials for viral replication.')],statements='A2.3.3')
    B.add(c,'ahl','Envelope sensitivity as evidence','In a constructed test, treatment with a lipid-disrupting agent reduces infectivity of virus X by 95%, but affects a non-enveloped control little. Which inference is most justified?',correct='An intact lipid-associated structure is important for infectivity of X under the tested conditions.',wrong=['Every virus must possess a lipid envelope.','X must have a DNA genome rather than an RNA genome.','The agent has proven that X carries no protein.'],why='The result is consistent with envelope dependence, but it does not establish universal viral structure or genome type.',data=True,statements='A2.3.2')
    B.add(c,'ahl','Diversifying from a common mode of life','Viruses have very different genome types and structures but all depend on host cells to reproduce.',[P('Explain why similar host dependence does not by itself prove a single viral origin.','A similar parasitic mode of life could evolve independently in different lineages.'),P('Explain how viral diversity supports consideration of multiple origins.','Major differences in genomes and replication strategies are consistent with different ancestral sources or evolutionary routes.'),P('State one kind of evidence useful for comparing these hypotheses.','Comparisons of homologous viral genes/proteins and related host sequences could test ancestry claims.')],statements='A2.3.5')
    B.add(c,'ahl','Sequence change during serial sampling','Constructed viral sequences are sampled at one aligned region. Relative to the starting sequence, the sample after ten infection cycles differs at 2 of 100 sites and the sample after twenty cycles differs at 5 of 100 sites.',[P('Calculate the percentage of sites differing in the later sample.',r'$5/100\times100=5\%$.'),P('Explain why the increase is consistent with evolution.','Heritable sequence variation has accumulated across viral generations.'),P('Explain why these observations alone do not establish the mutation rate per replication.','Observed differences also reflect selection, population sampling and loss of variants; infection cycles can contain multiple replication events.')],data=True,statements='A2.3.6')
    B.add(c,'ahl','Selection of a resistant viral variant','Before exposure to an antiviral, a constructed HIV population contains a rare variant less affected by the drug. After exposure, this variant forms a much larger fraction.',[P('Explain the change without assuming the drug produced the needed mutation.','The variant already existed before treatment.','It reproduces more successfully than susceptible variants under the drug, increasing its proportion.'),P('State one implication for controlling rapidly evolving viruses.','A control measure targeting one vulnerable feature may lose effectiveness as resistant variants increase; credit the need to monitor evolution.')],data=True,statements='A2.3.6')
    B.add(c,'ahl','High output and short generation times','A virus produces many offspring in short infection cycles. Mutations occasionally occur during copying.',[P('Explain why these features can permit rapid evolution.','Many replication events generate opportunities for new variants.','Short generation times allow selection to change variant frequencies over many generations in a short chronological period.'),P('Explain why not every mutation spreads.','Many variants are neutral or harmful, can be lost by chance, or reproduce less successfully in the current environment.')],statements='A2.3.6')
    B.add(c,'ahl','Changing influenza antigens','A hypothetical influenza lineage accumulates heritable changes in a surface protein recognized by antibodies. Which consequence is most directly plausible?',correct='Some existing antibodies may bind less effectively to the changed protein.',wrong=['Existing antibodies must retain exactly the same affinity because the virus is still influenza.','A changed amino-acid sequence cannot alter the three-dimensional antigenic surface.','Antibodies raised against the earlier lineage must bind the changed protein more strongly.'],why='Sequence changes can alter antigenic shape and antibody recognition without removing viral dependence on host cells.',statements='A2.3.6')
    B.add(c,'ahl','Comparing two viral control targets','A constructed experiment tests compounds against a viral entry protein. Compound U binds one variable region; compound V binds a functionally constrained region. Variants altering the constrained region show poor entry even without treatment.',[P('Suggest why resistance to V may be more costly to the virus.','Changes that prevent V binding may also impair the protein’s essential entry function.'),P('Explain why this is not a guarantee that resistance cannot evolve.','Other mutations, compensatory changes or alternative pathways may still permit successful reproduction.'),P('State a useful follow-up test.','Compare replication or entry of multiple selected variants with and without each compound under matched conditions.')],data=True,statements='A2.3.6')
    B.add(c,'ahl','Distinguishing a host cell from its viruses','A bacterium contains 70S ribosomes and a circular chromosome, with phages attached to its outside.',[P('Explain why the ribosomes should not be labelled as structures inside a phage.','They are cellular protein-synthesis machinery located in the bacterial cytoplasm; the phage lacks its own ribosomes.'),P('Explain why the bacterial chromosome should not be assumed to be the phage genome.','The host and virus have separate hereditary systems, although a prophage can be integrated into a host chromosome during lysogeny.')],statements='A2.3.1-4')
    B.add(c,'ahl','Evaluating a rapid-evolution claim','A constructed report compares the mutation frequencies of two viruses but samples one only after strong selection and the other before selection.',[P('Explain the flaw in attributing a frequency difference solely to copying accuracy.','Selection changes which variants survive and reproduce, so post-selection frequencies do not measure mutation generation alone.'),P('Suggest a more informative design.','Measure newly arising variants under comparable replication and selection conditions for both viruses.','Use multiple independent populations and the same sequencing/sampling procedures to estimate variability and reduce method bias.')],data=True,statements='A2.3.6')
